## Report
Rubric-driven validation completed. Crash attempt: `cargo test -p server-core --lib` failed because libisal was missing; rebuilt with `cargo test -p server-core --lib --no-default-features --features "null-ec,pure-rust-crc"` and tests passed (no crash). Valgrind not available (`valgrind: command not found`). Debugger attempt: `lldb -b -o "run coordinator::tests::get_object_rejects_private_read_for_non_owner --exact"` executed a single test (no crash; backtrace unavailable because process exited). Code analysis shows the authorization bypass: (1) Object reads allow access when requester matches object owner identity (authz.rs:142-166). (2) Object ACL read/write checks also allow access if requester matches owner identity (authz.rs:203-227). (3) Object tag management relies on `requester_can_manage_object_tags`, which grants access when requester matches owner identity (authz.rs:352-359), and the bucket-policy wrapper delegates to that check (authz.rs:797-813). (4) Anonymous requesters are treated as matching a special anonymous owner identity: `requester_matches_owner_identity` returns true for any anonymous requester if the owner is the anonymous-upload principal/canonical id (authz.rs:229-236). (5) Anonymous uploads are assigned that owner identity: `requester_owner_identity` returns `OwnerIdentity::anonymous_upload()` for anonymous requesters (authz.rs:1276-1279), used by `effective_object_owner` (authz.rs:1288-1301). During put-object commit, owner is set via `effective_put_object_owner` (coordinator.rs:7974-7976), which uses that anonymous owner identity. The shared anonymous owner identity is defined in storage/types.rs (types.rs:1240-1264). Therefore any anonymous requester is treated as the owner of any anonymously uploaded object, bypassing intended ACL isolation for read/ACL/tag operations.

